# arXiv:1806.11463 — Bayesian Deep Learning on a Quantum Computer

**Zhikuan Zhao, Alejandro Pozas-Kerstjens, Patrick Rebentrost, Peter Wittek** (v3, 2019).

## Extraction status

This is a **placeholder** Nougat markdown-math extraction stub. The
replication of arXiv:1806.11463 was driven from the arXiv PDF and
the paper's own textual description of the HHL protocol; a full
Nougat pass over the PDF was **not required** to reproduce the
numerical claims, because the entire testable content of the paper's
empirical section reduces to:

- one $2\times 2$ matrix $A = \tfrac{1}{2}\begin{pmatrix}3&1\\1&3\end{pmatrix}$,
- the HHL protocol applied to $A$ (paper Sec. IV.A),
- a claimed hardware fidelity $F=0.78$ on IBMQX5 (paper Fig. 3),
- and a claimed reduction of GP posterior inference to the linear
  solve $(K + \sigma_n^2 I)^{-1} v$ (paper Sec. II--III).

All four of those points are captured verbatim in `report/REPORT.md`
and are the direct source of the two Python drivers in `code/`.

## Key equations (extracted for reference)

GP posterior mean and variance:
$$\mu(x_*) = k_*^T (K + \sigma_n^2 I)^{-1} y$$
$$\sigma^2(x_*) = k(x_*, x_*) - k_*^T (K + \sigma_n^2 I)^{-1} k_*$$

Paper's benchmark matrix:
$$A = \frac{1}{2}\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 1 \\ 1 & 3 \end{pmatrix} = H \, \mathrm{diag}(2, 1) \, H$$
where $H$ is the Hadamard --- this is the structural fact our shallow
4-qubit HHL variant exploits.

HHL controlled-rotation angles for the paper's $A$ (with $C=1$):
$$\theta(\lambda=1) = 2 \arcsin(1) = \pi,\quad
  \theta(\lambda=2) = 2 \arcsin(1/2) = \pi/3.$$

Paper's headline hardware result:
$$P_{\text{swap-test, IBMQX5}} = 0.89 \Rightarrow F = 0.78.$$

